--- body ---
## Context

Follow-up to tracker 1507 (closed 2026-11-10). `scripts/trace-compact-audit.py`
now reads Claude Code, Codex and Pi sessions, on the host it runs on only, and
says so ("Host: padme (this host's corpora only; other hosts are not read)").
The ceilings bind every host. Asked whether the count means this host or the
fleet, the author ruled on 2026-11-10: "Audit the fleet by default, if the
other machine is up."

Observed on padme, 2026-11-10:

- `ssh -o BatchMode=yes doudou` answers; doudou has `~/.claude/projects`,
  `~/.codex/sessions`, `~/.pi/agent/sessions` and its own archive root
  (`~/data/datasets/ours/agent-sessions`).
- padme's archive holds a compressed copy of doudou's transcripts,
  `<archive>/raw/doudou/{claude,codex,pi,seat-raw}`: 28272 files, the newest
  dated 2026-11-08 (`scripts/transcript_archive.py` skips a source touched
  within a day, hence a lag).
- No list of fleet hosts was found in `scripts/` or the checks configuration;
  the archive's `raw/<host>` directories name doudou and padme.

## Scope

- By default the audit covers every host of the fleet, the local one and each
  other host that is up, and its table and JSON count sessions per host as
  well as per runtime.
- A host that is down, unreachable or unreadable is reported as could not
  look, never as zero sessions; the local counts are still given.
- An option limits the audit to the local host.
- Only aggregates cross hosts: no transcript content leaves the host it sits
  on, as the script's docstring already promises for its own output.

Option not taken, for the record: reading the other host's archive copy on
this host. It works when the host is down and needs no ssh, but lags (two
days on 2026-11-10). The author asked for the live reading when the host is
up.

Out of scope: any other trace script (ticket 1517); a fleet inventory beyond
what this audit needs; fixing clock or version drift between hosts beyond
reporting it.

## Test

With the remote reading replaced by a stub: a session past its ceiling on the
other host is counted under that host; a host that does not answer is named as
could not look while the local counts stand and the exit code is 0; the
local-only option reads no other host; an answer from another host that this
version cannot interpret (an older script there) is reported as could not
look, not merged.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Run with no option on a host of the fleet, the audit counts the sessions
  of every host that is up, per host and per runtime, and names each host
  read
- [ ] A host that is down or unreadable is reported as could not look, with a
  non-zero exit only when no corpus on any host is readable
- [ ] An option restricts the audit to the local host
- [ ] No transcript content crosses hosts: the other host returns aggregates
  only
- [ ] `make check` passes
